    "lawCode": "Amd §§445-d & 604-c, R & SS L",
    "lawSection": "Retirement and Social Security Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S5744\n\nSPONSOR: GOUNARDES\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the retirement and social security law, in relation to\nparticipation in certain retirement plans by active and retired members\nand staff of the New York city council\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nTo reopen the 55/25 plan to all employees of the New York City Council\nwho were eligible to join upon the plan's enactment but failed to file a\ntimely application through no fault of their own.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one of this bill amends subdivision a of section 445-d of the\nRetirement and Social Security Law (RSSL) to define active and retired\nNew York City Council employees. This is the section of RSSL which\ncovers Tier 2 members.\n\nSection two of this bill amends subdivision b of section 445-d of the\nRetirement and Social Security Law to exempt New York City Council\nemployees who were in active service upon the enactment of Chapter 96 of\nthe Laws of 1995 from the requirement to have filed an application for\nparticipation in the 55/25 Retirement Plan within 90 days of such plan's\nenactment.\n\nSection three of this bill amends subdivision c of section 445-d of the\nRetirement and Social Security Law to exempt New York City Council\nemployees who were in active service upon the enactment of Chapter 96 of\nthe Laws of 1995 from the requirement to have filed an application for\nparticipation in the 55/25 Retirement Plan within 90 days of such plan's\nenactment and to set the additional member contributions required to be\npaid to now join such plan.\n\nSection four of this bill amends subdivision a of section 604-c of the\nRetirement and Social Security Law to define active and retired New York\nCity Council employers. This is the section of RSSL which covers Tier 4\nmembers.\n\nSection five of this bill amends subdivision b of section 604-c of the\nRetirement and Social Security Law to exempt New York City Council\nemployees who were in active service upon the enactment of Chapter 96 of\nthe Laws of 1995 from the requirement to have filed an application for\nparticipation in the 55/25 Retirement Plan within 90 days of such plan's\nenactment.\n\nSection six of this bill amends subdivision c of section 604-c of the\nRetirement and Social Security Law to exempt New York City Council\nemployees who were in active service upon the enactment of Chapter 96 of\nthe Laws of 1995 from the requirement to have filed an application for\nparticipation in the 55/25 Retirement Plan within 90 days of such plan's\nenactment and to set the additional member contributions required to be\npaid to now join such plan.\n\nSection seven of this bill exempts it from the appropriation require-\nments of section 25 of RSSL.\nSection eight of this bill provides the effective date and that the\nNYCERS Board of Trustees shall issue an application to all City Council\nmembers newly eligible to join the 55/25 plan as a result of this act.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nOn June 28th, 1995, the New York State legislature created the New York\nCity Employees Retirement System (NYCERS) 55/25 Plan for all those who\nfiled an application within 90 days of the plan's enactment. Huge\nportions of the NYC workforce, including virtually all members and staff\nof the City Council, were never made aware of this filing period, howev-\ner, due to extremely poor outreach on the part of the City. No employ-\nment program or procedure existed at the time of their hiring providing\ninformation related to the 55/25 option, and so these employees were\nunjustly denied the opportunity to retire early by joining the 55/25.\nThose left behind in the 62/5 plan were forced to take a permanent\nreduction of their pensions as high as 27% if they tried to leave at age\n55.\n\nThis legislation seeks to redress this oversight by allowing all New\nYork City Council members and staff who were eligible to join the 55/25\nplan on June 28th, 1995 but failed to file a timely application to now\ndo so. Members would be required to make additional member contrib-\nutions, consistent with those required in the initial 1995 enactment,\nranging from 1.85% to 4.35% of gross wages depending on dates of\nservice. Additionally, members who did not join the 55/25 and then\nretired with at least 25 years of service would be eligible to have\ntheir pensions recalculated and a retroactive rebate issued for the\ndifference between their current benefit and what their benefit would\nhave been if they had joined such plan.\n\nThis bill seeks to hold harmless New York City Council employees who\nwere eligible to opt into the 55/25 but were never made aware of such\nplan's existence and thus failed to timely file. These dedicated civil\nservants and their families should not be forced to pay the price for\nthe City's extremely poor communication at the time of this plan's\nenactment.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2022: S6422A - Referred to Civil Service & Pensions\n\n2021: S6422 - Referred to Civil Service & Pensions\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nSee bill\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the ninetieth day after which it shall\nhave become a law.\n",
